ut  women we also don't know enough about  minorities  it's basically been done in a  homogeneous population so you take a  look at a pill we know that women are  obviously different from men on the  outside but they're different on the  inside yes we are  yes you are and then that's good vive la  différence but you got to understand the  difference and you take a pill you you  swallow it  well gastric acid is actually lower in  women than in men and there are other  factors that make it so that the  absorption is different then you think  about how does it get distributed  throughout the body well women have a  higher percentage of body fat they tend  to be smaller they tend to have less  blood volume less blood plasma and so  the way that it gets distributed  throughout the body is different then  you get to the target area the cell  itself where the medicines taking effect  there are differences by there even on a  cellular level they're generated by  hormones and by the way throughout the  menstrual cycle the hormones change and  in pregnancy something you may be aware  after menopause so there are all these  different areas and then finally how  quickly does the body get rid of the  medication how does it metabolism in the  liver excreted in the kidneys that has a  difference by sex awesome Wow okay so  the review also looked at the difference  in treatment in treatment what did it  find there and we tend to we've known  this too that we tend to under treat  women when it comes to cardiovascular  disease so we do less prevention less  preventive medicines when they need  medicines for treatment we tend to under  do that when they need procedures we  under do that and we also they know  there's been a big education push to  teach people that women can have  atypical symptoms when it comes to heart  disease right they don't have to have  the crushing chest pain they can have a  little bit of nausea a little bit of  fatigue and things like that so doc  short of getting more female lab rats  what do we need to do to improve our  understanding of the gender differences  well we do need to have more female drug  rats and we also need to have more women  in these trials so the NIH I have to say  you have to give them credit they've  made a big push for this and it's  actually mandated by law that they  include more more women in these studies  have more diversity and they're trying  to do that so you include them in the  trials and in addition you have to think  about how you report it out because when  you report it out you can't just say  here's the result - break it down by  gender right and hopefully in  in subgroups to say this is what happens  with women this is with minorities etc  etc so we still have a long way to go
 

